DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ttffttppdd utility is a server which supports the Internet Trivial File
     Transfer Protocol (RFC 1350).  The TFTP server operates at the port indi‐
     cated in the ‘tftp’ service description; see services(5).  The server is
     normally started by inetd(8).

     The use of tftp(1) does not require an account or password on the remote
     system.  Due to the lack of authentication information, ttffttppdd will allow
     only publicly readable files to be accessed.  Files containing the string
     “/../” or starting with “../” are not allowed.  Files may be written only
     if they already exist (unless the --ww option is used) and are publicly
     writable (unless chrooted and the --SS option is used).  Note that this ex‐
     tends the concept of “public” to include all users on all hosts that can
     be reached through the network; this may not be appropriate on all sys‐
     tems, and its implications should be considered before enabling tftp ser‐
     vice.  The server should have the user ID with the lowest possible privi‐
     lege.

     Access to files may be restricted by invoking ttffttppdd with a list of direc‐
     tories by including up to 20 pathnames as server program arguments in
     inetd.conf(5).  In this case access is restricted to files whose names
     are prefixed by the one of the given directories.  The given directories
     are also treated as a search path for relative filename requests.

     The --ss option provides additional security by changing the root directory
     of ttffttppdd, thereby prohibiting accesses to outside of the specified
     _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y.  Because chroot(2) requires super-user privileges, ttffttppdd must
     be run as root.  However, after performing the chroot(2) call, ttffttppdd will
     set its user ID to that of the specified _u_s_e_r, or “nobody” if no --uu op‐
     tion is specified.

     The options are:

     --bb      By default, ttffttppdd expects an initial message to be available on
             its input socket.  If no data is available, the server exits im‐
             mediately.  If --bb is specified, ttffttppdd will block waiting for the
             initial message.

     --cc      Changes the default root directory of a connecting host via
             chroot(2) based on the connecting IP address.  This prevents mul‐
             tiple clients from writing to the same file at the same time.  If
             the directory does not exist, the client connection is refused.
             The --ss option is required for --cc and the specified _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y is
             used as a base.

     --CC      Operates the same as --cc except it falls back to _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y speci‐
             fied via --ss if a directory does not exist for the client's IP.

     --FF      Use this strftime(3) compatible format string for the creation of
             the suffix if --WW is specified.  By default the string "%Y%m%d" is
             used.

     --dd,, --dd _[_v_a_l_u_e_]
             Enables debug output.  If _v_a_l_u_e is not specified, then the debug
             level is increased by one for each instance of --dd which is speci‐
             fied.

             If _v_a_l_u_e is specified, then the debug level is set to _v_a_l_u_e.  The
             debug level is a bitmask implemented in
             _s_r_c_/_l_i_b_e_x_e_c_/_t_f_t_p_d_/_t_f_t_p_-_u_t_i_l_s_._h.  Valid values are 0 (DEBUG_NONE),
             1 (DEBUG_PACKETS), 2, (DEBUG_SIMPLE), 4 (DEBUG_OPTIONS), and 8
             (DEBUG_ACCESS).  Multiple debug values can be combined in the
             bitmask by logically OR'ing the values.  For example, specifying
             --dd _1_5 will enable all the debug values.

     --ll      Log all requests using syslog(3) with the facility of LOG_FTP.
             NNoottee: Logging of LOG_FTP messages must also be enabled in the
             syslog configuration file, syslog.conf(5).

     --nn      Suppress negative acknowledgement of requests for nonexistent
             relative filenames.

     --oo      Disable support for RFC2347 style TFTP Options.

     --ss _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y
             Cause ttffttppdd to change its root directory to _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y.  After do‐
             ing that but before accepting commands, ttffttppdd will switch creden‐
             tials to an unprivileged user.

     --SS      If ttffttppdd runs chrooted, the option allows write requests accord‐
             ing to generic file permissions, skipping requirement for files
             to be publicly writable.  The option is ignored for non-chrooted
             run.

     --uu _u_s_e_r
             Switch credentials to _u_s_e_r (default “nobody”) when the --ss option
             is used.  The user must be specified by name, not a numeric UID.

     --UU _u_m_a_s_k
             Set the _u_m_a_s_k for newly created files.  The default is 022
             (S_IWGRP | S_IWOTH).

     --ww      Allow write requests to create new files.  By default ttffttppdd re‐
             quires that the file specified in a write request exist.  Note
             that this only works in directories writable by the user speci‐
             fied with --uu option

     --WW      As --ww but append a YYYYMMDD.nn sequence number to the end of the
             filename.  Note that the string YYYYMMDD can be changed with the
             --FF option.

